  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Considering Texture Rendering**\n\nI\'m currently focused on improving the visual representation of swatches 01-40. Instead of the existing colored boxes in `TextureCatalogue.tsx` and `swatches.ts`, I\'m exploring the creation of a dedicated `SwatchCanvas` component, or perhaps using SVGs or a canvas-based macro texture patch to render realistic texture grains.\n\n\n**Analyzing Visual Representations**\n\nI\'ve been examining the PDF sample card to understand the textures described on pages 3 and 4, specifically swatches 01 through 12. My goal is to determine how to create realistic visual representations of these textures. It seems important to capture the specifics of each texture (e.g., trowel grooves, metallic sheen, spray droplets, granular particles) so they appear like the actual material samples. I\'m focusing on the visual characteristics now.\n\n\n**Cataloging Sample Textures**\n\nI\'m now cataloging the remaining swatch descriptions. I\'ve compiled detailed notes on swatches 13 through 40, capturing the color and granular nature of each. My goal is to use this information to create visual representations. I\'m imagining the different ways to depict the details of these varying materials. I feel prepared to move forward.\n\n\n**Implementing Swatch Patches**\n\nI\'m now implementing the `<SwatchPatch />` component, aiming for photorealistic material representations. The approach involves using either HTML5 Canvas or high-performance SVG patterns. I\'ll dynamically generate realistic textures based on the swatch code, category, and color. I\'m focusing on rendering specific textural qualities: troweled grooves for Rustic, metallic shimmer for Parlina, sprayed droplets for Spray/Ultra Spray, micro-roller grain for Super Fine/Compact, smooth microcement for Concrete/Fairface, and crushed quartz for Granules/Quartz.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
